      "text": "The mean score of the first group of students was 72%, so the total score of these students is 72% * 30 = 2160%\n\nThe mean score of the second group of students was 78%, so the total score of these students is 78% * 6 = 468%\n\nThe total score of all 36 students is 2160% + 468% = 2628%\n\nThe new mean score is the total score divided by the total number of students, so 2628% / 36 = 73%\n\nTo find the new class mean, we just need to express the result as a percent.\n\n\\(\\boxed{73}\\)\n\nWow, this seems simple now that you explain it. Do you have any other interesting problems to work on?\nI don't have personal interests or preferences in the way that humans do. however, here are some interesting math problems that you might find interesting:\n\n- what is the probability that two randomly chosen natural numbers are coprime?\n- what is the maximum number of pairwise squares that can be used to tile a rectangle of size $m \\times n$, where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime?\n- given a set of $n$ distinct integers, what is the expected value of the product of their products?\n\nthese are just a few examples of interesting math problems that you might find fun to work on.\n\nCan you provide me with more details on how to approach the first question about finding the probability that two randomly chosen natural numbers are coprime?\nSure, finding the probability that two randomly chosen natural numbers are coprime is a well-known problem in number theory, and there are several approaches to solving it. \n\nOne approach is to use the Euler's Totient Function, denoted as $\\phi(n)$. The result is that the probability is given by $(\\phi(n))/(n)$. Here, $n$ is the product of the two numbers being compared. Thus, if $n$ and $m$ are two randomly chosen natural numbers, the probability that they are coprime is $\\phi(nm)/nm$.\n\nAnother approach to solving this problem is to use the fact that there are infinitely many prime numbers and that any number greater than $1$ is either a prime or a product of prime numbers. Suppose that we want to find the probability that two randomly selected integers are coprime. Then, the probability is zero if either of the two numbers are $1$. Otherwise, the probability is $\\phi(nm)/nm$, where the product $nm$ is the product of the two numbers. \n\nThere are also advanced proofs for this result, using techniques from number theory such as the Prime Number Theorem and Euler's asymptotic formula. However, these proofs are considered more challenging and typically require advanced techniques of number theory.",
